    ('f523b5df-b6a1-4dd9-ab12-59e22b0c7ea0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'From the First Dynasty to the Twenty-sixth Dynasty, nine or ten temples were successively built on one site at Abydos.', 'On one site at Abydos, the Osiris Temple rose as nine or ten temples in turn from the First Dynasty on. When the old temple vanished, a smaller one enclosed a wide hearth of black ashes.', 'teaser-card-s026:f523b5df-b6a1-4dd9-ab12-59e22b0c7ea0:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'ce9ded254effc28b2828fa3055d178bcd71a338653620b96dcdb2b1cd48cbeec|99e4c6166ebe5903a2140b4788f5a7dcb44fd908c328bfe24a6c16ddef0e25cc|99e4c6166ebe5903a2140b4788f5a7dcb44fd908c328bfe24a6c16ddef0e25cc', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On one site at Abydos, the Osiris Temple rose as nine or ten temples in turn from the First Dynasty on. When the old temple vanished, a smaller one enclosed a wide hearth of black ashes."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the temple stands at Abydos [S3]; nine or ten temples were built in succession on one site at Abydos [S3]; the succession began in the First Dynasty [S3]; the old temple vanished [S8]; a smaller building followed it [S8]; the smaller building enclosed a wide hearth of black ashes [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-085 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Osiris Temple is a temple at Abydos, Egypt (Great Osiris Temple) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abydos,_Egypt]; Nine or ten temples were successively built on one site at Abydos, from the First Dynasty on [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abydos,_Egypt]; The temples were built on one site, starting from the First Dynasty [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abydos,_Egypt]; The old temple vanished [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abydos,_Egypt]; A smaller building was erected after the old temple vanished [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abydos,_Egypt]; When the old temple vanished, a smaller one was built that enclosed a wide hearth of black ashes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abydos,_Egypt]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f53118e0-6771-41bb-8187-d7cee5a24a99'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Built in 123 AD, just after Hadrian''s Wall was completed, this cavalry fort at Chesters is the best-preserved Roman cavalry fort along the wall. Its bathhouse survives nearly complete.', 'Cilurnum, considered the best-preserved Roman cavalry fort on Hadrian''s Wall, pushed its walls north of the Wall, with three main gateways on the north for rapid access to that side.', 'teaser-card-s026:f53118e0-6771-41bb-8187-d7cee5a24a99:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '981795a28339403255ba433b738c491b066221e3e7ae9f40518bc8019ff9c4c7|dfbcbda9900e8059d51f88ceec481540230d5d03530a90e09fb3e3c6ec5be512|dfbcbda9900e8059d51f88ceec481540230d5d03530a90e09fb3e3c6ec5be512', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cilurnum, considered the best-preserved Roman cavalry fort on Hadrian''s Wall, pushed its walls north of the Wall, with three main gateways on the north for rapid access to that side."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cilurnum is a Roman fort on Hadrian''s Wall [S1]; it is considered the best-preserved Roman cavalry fort on Hadrian''s Wall [S3]; its walls projected north of the Wall [S4]; three of its main gateways were on the north side [S4]; the northern gateways allowed rapid access to that side [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-085 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cilurnum is a Roman fort on Hadrian''s Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cilurnum]; Cilurnum is considered the best-preserved Roman cavalry fort on Hadrian''s Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cilurnum]; Cilurnum was a cavalry fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cilurnum]; Its walls projected north of the Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cilurnum]; Three main gateways on the north allowed rapid access to that side [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cilurnum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f56ab010-efda-4068-a979-f935d9ab521f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'An inscription, recording the enshrinement of relics as a gift to the Sarvastivadin School, was found in a chaitya hall mentioning the date of "the 134th year of Azes," which corresponds to 77 CE.', 'At Kalawan, an image shrine faces the monastery entrance, a plan thought to have become the prototype for monasteries at Ajanta, Ellora and Nalanda. A relic inscription dates to 77 CE.', 'teaser-card-s026:f56ab010-efda-4068-a979-f935d9ab521f:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 184 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '549c736dede66493d24e2f78eec2486477369800ef8ff9cfd4d36e8f253b0407|12f3cea64647277fa147331486c344f12649c9cbd9653d8dc327f31fbcda9bde|12f3cea64647277fa147331486c344f12649c9cbd9653d8dc327f31fbcda9bde', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Kalawan, an image shrine faces the monastery entrance, a plan thought to have become the prototype for monasteries at Ajanta, Ellora and Nalanda. A relic inscription dates to 77 CE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kalawan has a monastery [S1, S3]; an image shrine is built opposite the monastery entrance [S3]; this plan is thought to have become the prototype for monasteries at Ajanta, Ellora and Nalanda [S4]; an inscription recording the enshrinement of relics was found there [S2]; the inscription dates to 77 CE [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-085 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kalawan is an archaeological (Buddhist monastery) site in Pakistan, near Taxila [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalawan]; An image shrine is built opposite the monastery entrance at Kalawan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalawan]; This plan is thought to have become the prototype for later monasteries such as Ajanta, Ellora and Nalanda [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalawan]; The site has a vihara monastery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalawan]; A relic inscription found at Kalawan dates to 77 CE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalawan]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f69c8107-a8fe-45ae-a8d8-d5ab8e618e33'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In 1899, eleven of the massive columns of the Great Hypostyle Hall collapsed in a chain reaction, because their foundations were undermined by ground water.', 'In the Precinct of Amon-Re at Karnak, the Hypostyle Hall covers 5,000 m2. In 1899, their foundations undermined by ground water, eleven of its massive columns collapsed in a chain reaction.', 'teaser-card-s026:f69c8107-a8fe-45ae-a8d8-d5ab8e618e33:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'e6dd213eecebc2af8fe972361dd7102ed463b8882596f3d01896c1bbd6e57dd7|c248a4c59bacab85784ffb25182f7f3ad196a1c29e1a133032843278f48c5bc6|c248a4c59bacab85784ffb25182f7f3ad196a1c29e1a133032843278f48c5bc6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Precinct of Amon-Re at Karnak, the Hypostyle Hall covers 5,000 m2. In 1899, their foundations undermined by ground water, eleven of its massive columns collapsed in a chain reaction."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Hypostyle Hall is in the Precinct of Amon-Re at Karnak [S1]; it covers 5,000 m2 [S3]; in 1899 eleven of its massive columns collapsed [S4]; the collapse was a chain reaction [S4]; the columns'' foundations had been undermined by ground water [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-085 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Hypostyle Hall is a hall in the Precinct of Amun-Re at Karnak, Egypt [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karnak]; The Hypostyle Hall covers 5,000 m2 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Hypostyle_Hall]; In 1899 eleven of its massive columns collapsed in a chain reaction [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Hypostyle_Hall]; Eleven columns collapsed (in 1899) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Hypostyle_Hall]; The collapse was because their foundations were undermined by ground water [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Hypostyle_Hall]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f6d1da2a-f957-47fb-9ccf-58f2197eacc8'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A fortress atop a monolithic rock that controlled the only land route into the Peloponnese. Its natural spring meant defenders could hold out through any siege.', 'With its secure water supply, Acrocorinth commanded the Isthmus of Corinth as a last line of defense. In myth, Briareus awarded the Isthmus to Poseidon and the acropolis to Helios.', 'teaser-card-s026:f6d1da2a-f957-47fb-9ccf-58f2197eacc8:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 180 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '059df979c6e51e38c15b95ea849a1fe87ff76abeb7a117120ff2dbe73fe8e4c0|bd8ec7be35bf0196b56fb4e9b17cc0ddd3197a373950e64bb6208416efcde9f7|bd8ec7be35bf0196b56fb4e9b17cc0ddd3197a373950e64bb6208416efcde9f7', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "With its secure water supply, Acrocorinth commanded the Isthmus of Corinth as a last line of defense. In myth, Briareus awarded the Isthmus to Poseidon and the acropolis to Helios."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Acrocorinth had a secure water supply [S1]; Acrocorinth commanded the Isthmus of Corinth [S1]; it served as a last line of defense [S1]; in a myth, Briareus gave a verdict in a dispute [S4]; the verdict awarded the Isthmus to Poseidon [S4]; the verdict awarded the acropolis of Corinth to Helios [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-085 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Acrocorinth is a fortified acropolis rock above ancient Corinth, Greece [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acrocorinth]; With its secure water supply Acrocorinth''s fortress was repeatedly a last line of defense because it commanded the Isthmus of Corinth [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acrocorinth]; Acrocorinth is a fortress (acropolis) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acrocorinth]; Acrocorinth commanded the Isthmus of Corinth [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acrocorinth]; In myth Briareus awarded the Isthmus to Poseidon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acrocorinth]; Briareus awarded the acropolis to Helios [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acrocorinth]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f75a7bb2-603b-4d3a-858f-88847af51793'::uuid, 'card_description', 'From 1200 BC, this 2.93-hectare fort near Hagley has two ramparts and a sacred spring called St. Anthony''s Well. Local tradition holds it as the Dobunni tribe''s last stand against Rome.', 'Two sets of ramparts and ditches ring Wychbury Ring, an Iron Age hill fort on Wychbury Hill. Small bronze rings, among them an Iron Age terret, were found inside the fort in 1884.', 'teaser-card-s026:f75a7bb2-603b-4d3a-858f-88847af51793:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 179 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '69b8381317bb14e6de11835516e9b2d7f377dea7512e309ede8165145bc7d6e9|627f2e6631c454f1f059577f4c2e91e3655203e2e90ce9ac38af0abc2e2d8e2d|627f2e6631c454f1f059577f4c2e91e3655203e2e90ce9ac38af0abc2e2d8e2d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Two sets of ramparts and ditches ring Wychbury Ring, an Iron Age hill fort on Wychbury Hill. Small bronze rings, among them an Iron Age terret, were found inside the fort in 1884."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wychbury Ring has two sets of ramparts and ditches [S3]; Wychbury Ring is an Iron Age hill fort [S1]; it lies on Wychbury Hill [S1]; small bronze rings were found in the fort [S7]; among them was an Iron Age terret [S7]; they were found in 1884 [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-086 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wychbury Ring is an Iron Age hill fort on Wychbury Hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wychbury_Ring]; It is in England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wychbury_Ring]; It has two sets of ramparts and ditches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wychbury_Ring]; Small bronze rings, among them an Iron Age terret, were found inside the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wychbury_Ring]; The rings include an Iron Age terret [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wychbury_Ring]; The fort stands on Wychbury Hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wychbury_Ring]; The fort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wychbury_Ring]; The bronze rings were found in 1884 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wychbury_Ring]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f7625adc-de9a-4628-90d2-4200314e7f80'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Midhowe Chambered Cairn is a large Neolithic chambered cairn located on the south shore of the island of Rousay, Orkney.', 'On the south shore of Rousay, Midhowe Chambered Cairn lines a passage 23.6 metres long with twelve chambers, split by transverse stones that reminded early investigators of horse stalls.', 'teaser-card-s026:f7625adc-de9a-4628-90d2-4200314e7f80:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'fafe42581c97cf912b90d9e7a967d3f53f939b03112bc492ca8a24c362ea9b3a|b3f28712c1dffcb41a825366531d648f3565a2cf12819e3429390e4ccef7965d|b3f28712c1dffcb41a825366531d648f3565a2cf12819e3429390e4ccef7965d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the south shore of Rousay, Midhowe Chambered Cairn lines a passage 23.6 metres long with twelve chambers, split by transverse stones that reminded early investigators of horse stalls."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Midhowe Chambered Cairn lies on the south shore of Rousay [S1]; its passage is 23.6 metres long [S6]; twelve chambers flank the passage [S6]; the chambers are separated by transverse stones [S5, S6]; the stalled arrangement reminded early investigators of horse stalls [S4, S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-086 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Midhowe Chambered Cairn is a chambered cairn (tomb) on the south shore of Rousay [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midhowe_Chambered_Cairn]; It has twelve chambers flanking a passage 23.6 metres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midhowe_Chambered_Cairn]; The cairn has twelve chambers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midhowe_Chambered_Cairn]; The passage is 23.6 metres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midhowe_Chambered_Cairn]; The site is on the island of Rousay [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midhowe_Chambered_Cairn]; The chambers are split by transverse stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midhowe_Chambered_Cairn]; The transverse stones reminded early investigators of horse stalls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midhowe_Chambered_Cairn]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f7a58094-985b-4f4c-ad0e-c7d8b4c5c2f4'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Circular dwellings alongside rectangular chullpa burial towers at high altitude. Chullpas housed the dead above ground, as if they never truly left.', 'On the slopes of Pitaq Mach''ay in the Huánuco Region, Tikra holds circular buildings and square or rectangular burial towers, a site declared National Cultural Heritage in 2001.', 'teaser-card-s026:f7a58094-985b-4f4c-ad0e-c7d8b4c5c2f4:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 177 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '62c83881126ef03a19390ef8105c720799ef90ab3fd929773fe6d1448f5d5e71|72153c6bed1a7deb6f1424eb92f91204367efc3e65716526db7abc4e14006137|72153c6bed1a7deb6f1424eb92f91204367efc3e65716526db7abc4e14006137', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the slopes of Pitaq Mach''ay in the Huánuco Region, Tikra holds circular buildings and square or rectangular burial towers, a site declared National Cultural Heritage in 2001."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tikra lies on the slopes of Pitaq Mach''ay [S5]; Tikra is in the Huánuco Region [S4]; Tikra has circular buildings [S3]; Tikra has square or rectangular burial towers [S3]; Tikra was declared National Cultural Heritage [S2]; the declaration was made in 2001 [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-086 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tikra is an archaeological site in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tikra]; It lies on the slopes of Pitaq Mach''ay in the Huánuco Region [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tikra]; The site is in the Huánuco Region [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tikra]; The site has circular buildings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tikra]; The site has square or rectangular burial towers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tikra]; It consists of circular buildings and square or rectangular burial towers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tikra]; Declaration as National Cultural Heritage was on 20 September 2001 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tikra]; It was declared National Cultural Heritage in 2001 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tikra]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f859776a-9e93-4c62-9585-1f4aed2cbaa0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Roman baths of Varna were built towards the end of the 2nd century and remained in use for about a hundred years, till the late 3rd century.', 'Built in the late 2nd century AD, the Roman Thermae had an apodyterium, a frigidarium, a tepidarium, a caldarium and a palaestra, and stayed in use for about a hundred years.', 'teaser-card-s026:f859776a-9e93-4c62-9585-1f4aed2cbaa0:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 174 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '5aa81e2c42146529181ba06fac0c48584f5e5dae52332b9455aef0c9f544ae77|abdaadb2bdc1d67ac3278b82ff1ad65b582d49794e6af3102ebcc6716ff3fcf4|abdaadb2bdc1d67ac3278b82ff1ad65b582d49794e6af3102ebcc6716ff3fcf4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-rewrite-v-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Built in the late 2nd century AD, the Roman Thermae had an apodyterium, a frigidarium, a tepidarium, a caldarium and a palaestra, and stayed in use for about a hundred years."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-v-003 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the baths were built in the late 2nd century AD [S3, S4]; they had an apodyterium, frigidarium, tepidarium and caldarium [S6]; they had a palaestra [S6]; they remained in use for about a hundred years [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify2-008 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Thermae is a Roman bath complex in Varna, Bulgaria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Thermae_(Varna)]; Built in the late 2nd century AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Thermae_(Varna)]; It had an apodyterium, frigidarium, tepidarium, caldarium and a palaestra [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Thermae_(Varna)]; It stayed in use for about a hundred years [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Thermae_(Varna)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f91af2e3-60fc-4c63-9622-05c4e3f4b1cd'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A 6th-century BC quarry where tuff blocks for the Greek walls of Neapolis were cut. Alphabetical marks carved by ancient quarrymen are still visible on the rock faces.', 'Under a cemetery in Poggioreale, subsidence in 1987 revealed a vast quarry where tuff blocks for the Greek city wall of Neapolis were cut, still marked with the quarrymen''s letters.', 'teaser-card-s026:f91af2e3-60fc-4c63-9622-05c4e3f4b1cd:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '1951ece9296db805e29cb36d817064eaf0f9021226196ac888e61cccc84b6f98|dd327dd69b35fd8d4e19573cc11e8731d87e5faf6d81d3ed5e50ad97ab24ccb1|dd327dd69b35fd8d4e19573cc11e8731d87e5faf6d81d3ed5e50ad97ab24ccb1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Under a cemetery in Poggioreale, subsidence in 1987 revealed a vast quarry where tuff blocks for the Greek city wall of Neapolis were cut, still marked with the quarrymen''s letters."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is in Poggioreale [S1]; subsidence under a cemetery led to the quarry''s discovery [S1]; the discovery was made in 1987 [S1]; the quarry is vast [S1]; tuff blocks for the Greek city wall of Neapolis were cut there [S1]; the quarry still bears the ancient quarrymen''s alphabetical marks [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-086 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Site is a vast ancient tuff quarry in Poggioreale, Naples [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poggioreale,_Naples]; The quarry lies in Poggioreale (Naples) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poggioreale,_Naples]; The quarry is of tuff blocks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poggioreale,_Naples]; The wall was the Greek city wall of Neapolis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poggioreale,_Naples]; The tuff blocks were for the Greek city wall of Neapolis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poggioreale,_Naples]; The quarry was identified in 1987 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poggioreale,_Naples]; Subsidence under a cemetery revealed it [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poggioreale,_Naples]; The quarry was found in 1987 after subsidence under a cemetery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poggioreale,_Naples]; The quarry is marked with the quarrymen''s letters [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poggioreale,_Naples]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f9bb5abb-8ef2-4a21-adb5-2504fc9e87bb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Consisting of a roughly circular low earthen rampart surrounded by a ditch, Chanctonbury Ring is thought to date to the late Bronze Age or early Iron Age.', 'Atop the South Downs, the earthen rampart of Chanctonbury Ring was reused as a sacred precinct, where at least two Romano-Celtic temples were built, probably in the second century.', 'teaser-card-s026:f9bb5abb-8ef2-4a21-adb5-2504fc9e87bb:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 180 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'f909ee75a32ae8c6372e79e5ae4138b12600035878f6173c3e4aa294da37b51d|7e3c82c6b9db84fc32f3c2e83e30457b248b11d86bb41eef03e3d1ddc099ac53|7e3c82c6b9db84fc32f3c2e83e30457b248b11d86bb41eef03e3d1ddc099ac53', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Atop the South Downs, the earthen rampart of Chanctonbury Ring was reused as a sacred precinct, where at least two Romano-Celtic temples were built, probably in the second century."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chanctonbury Ring lies atop the South Downs [S1]; it has an earthen rampart [S2]; the bank and ditch were reused to form a sacred precinct [S7]; at least two Romano-Celtic temples were built there [S7]; the temples were probably built in the second century [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-087 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chanctonbury Ring is a prehistoric hill fort (an earthwork enclosure) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanctonbury_Ring]; It lies atop the South Downs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanctonbury_Ring]; It has an earthen rampart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanctonbury_Ring]; The rampart was reused as a sacred precinct [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanctonbury_Ring]; At least two temples were built there [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanctonbury_Ring]; The temples were of the Romano-Celtic type [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanctonbury_Ring]; The temples were built probably in the second century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanctonbury_Ring]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f9ccac76-d1c7-46c4-9c70-4748ce838527'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Roman-era agora from the 4th century BC. Agoras were the hearts of ancient cities—marketplace, courthouse, and public square in one.', 'Smyrna Agora Ancient City holds an agora first built by the Greeks in the 4th century BC. An earthquake ruined it in 178 AD, and the Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius ordered it rebuilt.', 'teaser-card-s026:f9ccac76-d1c7-46c4-9c70-4748ce838527:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '592bc237cdb1019869d37829bf4af9e6025e7aa02d1b5062374fae2347f78bd1|0e8d9032ee71691dc01fd0a91bbf00153393a0700f136c0fcf65948730696067|0e8d9032ee71691dc01fd0a91bbf00153393a0700f136c0fcf65948730696067', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Smyrna Agora Ancient City holds an agora first built by the Greeks in the 4th century BC. An earthquake ruined it in 178 AD, and the Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius ordered it rebuilt."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Smyrna Agora Ancient City holds an agora [S1]; the agora was first built by the Greeks [S2]; it was built in the 4th century BC [S2]; an earthquake ruined it in 178 AD [S2]; Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius ordered its reconstruction [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-087 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Smyrna Agora is an ancient agora [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agora_of_Smyrna]; It is located in Smyrna (present-day Izmir, Turkey) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agora_of_Smyrna]; The agora was first built by the Greeks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agora_of_Smyrna]; It was first built in the 4th century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agora_of_Smyrna]; An earthquake ruined it in 178 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agora_of_Smyrna]; The Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius ordered its rebuilding [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agora_of_Smyrna]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f9d17ed5-eb41-4332-a2c1-42d7d9b57a56'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A nearly 20-meter Roman monument originally clad in marble, erected in 9 BC for the general Drusus. Roman troops built it as a cenotaph after his sudden death.', 'In the citadel of Mainz rises the Drususstein, a nearly 20-metre-high Roman block once cased in marble, largely accepted as a remnant of the cenotaph raised in 9 BC for the general Drusus.', 'teaser-card-s026:f9d17ed5-eb41-4332-a2c1-42d7d9b57a56:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '7a3eaa0ac3f136244ded6d69bdab2147d09f9344702e193aa0343dee0913b196|c915c623dc6b492c58eefe7a67987de8bb31d6762c30ca68b35a41de61f2e604|c915c623dc6b492c58eefe7a67987de8bb31d6762c30ca68b35a41de61f2e604', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the citadel of Mainz rises the Drususstein, a nearly 20-metre-high Roman block once cased in marble, largely accepted as a remnant of the cenotaph raised in 9 BC for the general Drusus."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Drususstein is in the citadel of Mainz [S1]; it is nearly 20 metres high [S1]; it is a masonry block of Roman origin [S1]; it was once cased in marble [S2]; it is largely accepted as a remnant of a cenotaph [S3]; the cenotaph was erected in 9 BC [S3]; the cenotaph honoured the general Drusus [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-087 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Drususstein is a Roman masonry block in Mainz [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drususstein]; It stands in the citadel of Mainz [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drususstein]; It is nearly 20 metres high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drususstein]; It was once cased in marble [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drususstein]; It is largely accepted as a remnant of a cenotaph [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drususstein]; The cenotaph was raised in 9 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drususstein]; The cenotaph honoured the general Drusus [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drususstein]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('fa2075b9-ae78-4ce0-b00f-673d0139f0ff'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Home to petroglyphs left by the Hohokam people (200–1450 AD) alongside the iconic saguaro cacti. The carvings survive in one of America''s hottest deserts.', 'In Saguaro National Park, where the Hohokam lived in villages between AD 200 and 1450, the Signal Hill Trail leads to dozens of their petroglyphs, 800-year-old rock art etched into stone.', 'teaser-card-s026:fa2075b9-ae78-4ce0-b00f-673d0139f0ff:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 187 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '8c0e775e5816741ce3a6f97173f73dbff79b479539592375313ca67af9c67fdb|e4bd9aa493776a749faf5c290daa44c0979c58394ea5ef7a9ac6887c68604396|e4bd9aa493776a749faf5c290daa44c0979c58394ea5ef7a9ac6887c68604396', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-029", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Saguaro National Park, where the Hohokam lived in villages between AD 200 and 1450, the Signal Hill Trail leads to dozens of their petroglyphs, 800-year-old rock art etched into stone."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-029 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Hohokam lived in villages in the area of Saguaro National Park [S3]; they lived there between AD 200 and 1450 [S3]; the Signal Hill Trail in the park leads to dozens of petroglyphs [S6]; the petroglyphs are Hohokam work [S5, S6]; the rock art is 800 years old [S6]; the petroglyphs are etched into stone [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-087 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Saguaro National Park is a national park in Arizona, USA [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saguaro_National_Park]; The Hohokam lived in villages in the park area [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saguaro_National_Park]; The Hohokam lived there between AD 200 and 1450 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saguaro_National_Park]; The Signal Hill Trail leads to petroglyphs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saguaro_National_Park]; There are dozens of petroglyphs, which are Hohokam [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saguaro_National_Park]; The rock art is 800 years old [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saguaro_National_Park]; The petroglyphs are etched into stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saguaro_National_Park]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('fa63f634-1804-4382-9e66-e0e38c5d301a'::uuid, 'card_description', '2nd-1st-millennium BC fort, tower, and city wall traces in Laconian Sparta. Roof tiles and structural remains at Klaraki hint at a fortified hilltop settlement above the Mousga Stream.', 'A hill in Laconia beside the ridges of Bambakia, Klaraki still shows traces of a fort, a tower and a city wall, and archaeological searches there have turned up roof tiles.', 'teaser-card-s026:fa63f634-1804-4382-9e66-e0e38c5d301a:card_description', 'teaser-card-s026 (teaser-card): teaser card, 172 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '0094cd13cd2fb14591b2fc10fff7b6d16aac144ae8eddf92b311fa5db8dedf0f|7add584821e6ee3217cc65c7566e0bfe0d198e502cea39bdfd96453db204d614|7add584821e6ee3217cc65c7566e0bfe0d198e502cea39bdfd96453db204d614', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-write-030", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "A hill in Laconia beside the ridges of Bambakia, Klaraki still shows traces of a fort, a tower and a city wall, and archaeological searches there have turned up roof tiles."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-check-030 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Klaraki is a hill [S1]; it is in Laconia [S1]; it lies beside the ridges of Bambakia [S4]; there are traces of a fort [S2]; there are traces of a tower [S2]; there are traces of a city wall [S2]; archaeological searches there found roof tiles [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-05: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-05-verify-087 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-05/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Klaraki is a hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klaraki]; It is in Laconia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klaraki]; It lies beside the ridges of Bambakia [SUPPORTED: https:/